LIVE TALK UNITY 2.0 / genesisinteractive =E2=80=94 TEST NOW
Direct repo: https://github.com/genesisinteractive/LiveTalk-Unity
Latest verified repository activity: Sep 19, 2026. The current repo
documents a breaking 2.0 architecture.

WHAT CHANGED
LiveTalk-Unity now combines LivePortrait + MuseTalk + Qwen3-TTS behind
a local Unity/ONNX/CoreML character runtime. Version 2.0 replaces
Spark-TTS with Qwen3-TTS and, more importantly, decomposes a
persistent synthetic character into independently reusable Avatar,
Voice and Character entities rather than storing one monolithic
character bundle.

WHY THIS IS TECHNICALLY INTERESTING
This is less about novel generation quality than a very clean
persistent-character data model. Avatar assets are
immutable/content-addressed; cloned voices are content-addressed from
reference PCM + transcript; Character is only a lightweight reference
to an avatar and voice. Multiple characters can therefore share a
face, voices can be swapped without rebuilding visual assets, and
speech/lipsync caches are keyed on the actual reusable dependencies
rather than character ID. This is a strong implementation reference
for our Character Asset Graph.

INPUT -> OUTPUT
Portrait + designed/cloned voice + text/expression -> local generated
speech + streamed lip-synced expressive frames.

CODE / WEIGHTS / LICENSE
Code: runnable, MIT. LivePortrait and MuseTalk components: MIT.
Qwen3-TTS package/weights: Apache-2.0 per project documentation, with
upstream model cards still needing final shipping review. Pre-exported
ONNX LivePortrait/MuseTalk weights are provided separately; Qwen3-TTS
ONNX export tooling is in the companion Unity package.

HARDWARE / PERFORMANCE
Tested on Apple Silicon / MacBook Pro M4 Max with CoreML. Speech +
lipsync: 10=E2=80=9311 FPS generated. MuseTalk stage: 11=E2=80=9312 FPS. Li=
vePortrait
stage: ~4 FPS. Qwen3-TTS: ~0.97x realtime, ~1 s to first streamed
chunk, ~11 s cold checkpoint open. 32 GB RAM recommended for avatar
creation; 16 GB can work in Optimal mode with one TTS checkpoint
resident. Disk: ~7 GB LivePortrait+MuseTalk plus ~8 GB per Qwen3-TTS
checkpoint. Windows compiles but is untested.

MATURITY
RUNNABLE.

RELEVANCE
High as a persistent-character runtime/reference architecture;
moderate as our final renderer because 10=E2=80=9311 FPS is below realtime.
Particularly useful for local synthetic hosts, interactive characters,
Unity/3D scenes, and caching reusable character state.

EXACT EXPERIMENT
Create one canonical host once, then produce 50 utterances across 7
expressions and 3 voices. Measure cold character build, warm load,
first-audio latency, first-frame latency, sustained FPS, cache hit
speed, RAM, disk, identity score and lip-sync score. Then swap voices
without rebuilding the avatar and verify that cached visual state
remains reusable. Compare this persistent-asset lifecycle against our
current browser/VRM path.

REPLACE / COMPLEMENT
Do not replace the premium renderer. Use it as a reference/possible
backend for EDGE_RENDERER and directly borrow its asset
decomposition/caching semantics for Character Asset Graph.

DECISION
